## File Format Examples

### JSON Format

```json
{
  "identifier": "model",
  "name": "Vehicle Model",
  "type": "model",
  "author": "Engineer",
  "members": {
    "vehicle": {
      "identifier": "vehicle",
      "name": "Vehicle",
      "type": "class",
      "features": {
        "speed": {
          "identifier": "speed",
          "name": "speed",
          "type": "attribute",
          "feature_type": "Real",
          "multiplicity": "1"
        }
      }
    }
  },
  "owned_packages": {}
}
```

### XML Format

```xml
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<SysMLModel id="model" name="Vehicle Model" version="1.0" author="Engineer">
  <documentation>Model description</documentation>
  <Package id="pkg1" name="Package">
    <Class id="vehicle" name="Vehicle" visibility="public">
      <Feature id="speed" name="speed" visibility="public">
        <type>Real</type>
        <multiplicity>1</multiplicity>
      </Feature>
    </Class>
  </Package>
</SysMLModel>
```

## Extensibility

The parser is designed for extensibility:

1. **Custom Elements**: Extend `SysMLElement` to create custom element types
2. **Custom Serialization**: Override `to_dict()` for custom serialization
3. **Custom Parsing**: Extend parser methods for custom formats
4. **Metadata**: Use the `metadata` dictionary for application-specific data

## Requirements

- Python 3.8+
- Standard library only (no external dependencies)

